<article class="reading">
  <h1>How I Think</h1>

  <p>Good design makes people feel like they're in control, especially when the software is complicated and mistakes are costly.</p>

  <p>I've spent 25 years designing and building software, from sketch to production code, and I've led design teams as a player-coach.</p>

  <section class="how-i-think-section" aria-labelledby="design-heading">
    <h2 id="design-heading">My design process</h2>
    <p>AI does the production and I do the judgment. Here's my current approach:</p>

    {% include "components/think-process.njk" %}

    <p>Most of my time goes to deciding what the problem is, deciding what's good, and getting it shipped. AI speeds those parts up, but it can't do them for me.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="how-i-think-section" aria-labelledby="beliefs-heading">
    <h2 id="beliefs-heading">What I believe</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Design is decision-making.</strong> The ability to clarify tradeoffs, prioritize, and rally a team around an idea.</li>
      <li><strong>Slow down to go fast.</strong> AI makes it easy to build something polished, which also makes it easy to move <a href="https://www.seangood.win/articles/high-fidelity-low-definition">really fast in the wrong direction</a>. Unless the change is small, I get the problem right first.</li>
      <li><strong>Rough and directionally correct over beautiful and wrong.</strong> A good prototype shortens debates and gets real feedback fast.</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence beats opinions.</strong> I'd rather watch one customer struggle with a flow than hear ten opinions about what they'd probably want.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="/blog/product-design-process/">Process is a tool, not a religion</a>.</strong> The team and the problem define the process, not the other way around.</li>
      <li><strong>Know which doors are one-way.</strong> Most decisions are cheap to undo. A few aren't, and those are worth slowing down for.</li>
    </ul>

    {% include "components/think-doors.njk" %}
  </section>

  <section class="how-i-think-section" aria-labelledby="trust-heading">
    <h2 id="trust-heading">Designing AI that people trust</h2>
    <p>What should AI automate, and what shouldn't it? It depends on what a mistake costs.</p>

    <p>Forgetting the extra coffee on a DoorDash order is annoying. A payroll mistake or an error in legal paperwork can ruin someone's month.</p>

    {% include "components/think-spectrum.njk" %}

    <p>So I don't put an LLM everywhere. The design work is deciding how much freedom to give AI: when to let it loose, and when a fixed workflow or a person should take over.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="how-i-think-section" aria-labelledby="teams-heading">
    <h2 id="teams-heading">How I work across teams</h2>
    <p>At Dialpad, I inherited a feature factory. Every PM had their own roadmap, designers worked through queues of tickets, and engineers were boxed in by technical limits. The product looked like our org chart. We regrouped the work around shared themes, and teams started solving problems together instead of shipping features side by side. That's <a href="https://www.noidea.dog/glue">glue work</a>, and it's a big part of my job.</p>

    <p>I also work with teams like customer support, professional services, and sales. I'd rather hear about a problem early than in an escalation. When a customer asks for something specific, I look for the spirit of the ask and design it for everyone.</p>

    <p>Once work is underway:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>I bring PMs and engineers into the design process early, through brainstorms, diagrams, and wireframes, so everyone has input before we go too far in one direction. The best ideas don't always come from people with design in their title.</li>
      <li>When someone asks for the unreasonable, I lead with "<a href="https://www.intercom.com/blog/the-orange-juice-test">yes, and here's what it costs</a>." I explain what we'd delay or give up, and let people decide with full information.</li>
      <li>Teaching is part of the job. I've taught non-technical designers GitHub, set up a prototyping sandbox, and got designers and PMs using AI tools within six months.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="how-i-think-section" aria-labelledby="lead-heading">
    <h2 id="lead-heading">How I lead</h2>
    <p>When I first became a manager, I hired good people and stayed out of their way. Some thrived and some stalled, so I got more deliberate: notice what each person needs, coach them on it directly, and adjust as they grow.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Clear, actionable feedback.</strong> I don't sugarcoat critique or avoid hard conversations.</li>
      <li><strong>Designers should think like product people, not production artists.</strong> Craft gets you to senior. Storytelling, business sense, and selling your ideas get you further. I spent two years helping a senior designer make that shift before promoting them to staff.</li>
      <li><strong>Managers should be in the work.</strong> I owned features end to end, even as a people manager. It's hard to coach designers when you've stopped designing.</li>
    </ul>

  </section>

